  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 158 PM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nAfter the cold start Saturday morning, conditions begin to warm \nagain, with a return to seasonal to above seasonal highs and warmer \novernight lows. Upper level ridging begins to track across the \nregion again, building in earnest on Saturday. By Saturday \nafternoon, expecting highs to range from the lower 60s east of \nHighway 83 to the lower 70s west of Highway 61. With the warmer \nconditions out west, will be keeping an eye for potential elevated \nfire weather concerns during the afternoon hours, as the above \naverage temperatures will also usher in near critical humidity \nvalues. There may be sufficient overlap with gusty winds to cause a \nfew hours of concern, so will continue to monitor closely. By \nSaturday night, expecting warmer, but still cool, overnight lows \nranging from the upper 30s to lower 40s. \n\nSunday and beyond, with upper level ridging expected to be in place \nacross the region, expecting mostly warmer, dryer conditions through \nthe week. Highs Sunday and early in the week are expected to return \nto the mid 60s to lower 70s, with overnight lows in the lower 40s. \nFor reference, typical highs this time of year are around 62 to 64 \ndegrees, with typical overnight lows of 31 to 33 degrees. As ridging \ncontinues to build on Tuesday, we may see even warmer highs, \npotentially pushing into the mid to upper 70s by late week, with \npotential for overnight lows to return to the 50s. Guidance still \ncontinues to suggest a possible upper level disturbance mid to late \nweek, which may bring our next chances for precipitation, but there \nis still considerable uncertainty in track and timing.",
